      "text": "<img alt=\"\" src=\"assets\\ot3_0066_d50b41b36df6.png\" style=\"width: 175px; height: 180px;\" />",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<img alt=\"\" src=\"assets\\ot4_0066_dab7e8b0fa88.png\" style=\"width: 173px; height: 180px;\" />",
      "is_correct": true
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "D"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>In case of isothermal process, pressure vs volume graph is a rectangular hyperbola.</p><p>The graph of the isothermal process shifts away from the origin as the temperature of the process is increased. Thus, the curve away from the&nbsp;origin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math> have the highest temperature whereas the curve closer to the origin&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> have the lowest temperature.</p><p>Here, the dotted line shows&nbsp;the isobaric line that implies&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#62;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#62;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>P</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>R</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>.</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"assets\\st0066_ef55006a39d1.png\" style=\"width: 241px; height: 220px;\" /></p>"
  }
